# EVERY CHECKABLE ROW IN THE CORPUS, WITH ITS VERDICT. One row per (rule, examined item):
# which rule looked at it, which layer of which filing it is, whether it breaches the rule,
# and a sentence saying what it says.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THIS IS THE RELATION THE VIOLATIONS TABLE AND THE COVERAGE TABLE ARE BOTH VIEWS OF,
#    AND THAT IS THE ENTIRE POINT OF ASSEMBLING IT. A violations table is
#    `WHERE violates`; a coverage table is `GROUP BY rule`. Because they read ONE relation,
#    a coverage number cannot be about a different population than the rule it reports on --
#    which is exactly what used to happen: each population was written twice, in two dialects
#    of itself, and three of the twenty-two restatements disagreed. One of them counted four
#    rows where the rule examined one, crossing a threshold and reporting a thin rule as well
#    covered.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND IT IS THE MOST USEFUL THING HERE TO RUN ON ITS OWN. An empty violations table is
#    the good outcome and tells a reader nothing at all. THIS shows every rule working on real
#    rows -- what it looked at, what each row said, and why it passed -- which is the only way
#    to see that a rule is doing something rather than merely finding nothing.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND A RULE THAT EXAMINES NOTHING STILL APPEARS HERE, WITH A NULL VERDICT. That is not
#    a quirk; it is the reason every check starts by joining checks/roster.sqlc. A check that
#    merely filtered its population would vanish from a `GROUP BY` when the population was
#    empty -- so the one verdict that matters most, ⛔ VACUOUS, is precisely the one a grouping
#    cannot produce. The roster row exists before the population does, so the rule reports
#    that it ran even when it had nothing to look at.
#
# ⛔ THE CONTRACT EVERY CHECK KEEPS: one plain SELECT, no leading WITH, returning exactly
#   `(rule, filing, layer, violates, detail)`, with `violates` NULL only on the row that says
#   a rule examined nothing. A leading WITH cannot appear after UNION ALL, so a check needing
#   recursion puts the walk in its own relation under composition/ and joins it -- which is
#   better anyway, because the walk is then runnable by itself.
-- the conformance rules XSD 1.0 cannot reach, one file each; checks/roster.sqlc is the list.
:union(ALL checks/fit_disagrees.sqlc,
           checks/shares_do_not_sum.sqlc,
           checks/nobody_named_as_unserved.sqlc,
           checks/exposure_unaccounted.sqlc,
           checks/share_exceeds_slack.sqlc,
           checks/slack_unit_mismatch.sqlc,
           checks/quantum_unit_mismatch.sqlc,
           checks/nameplate_not_a_multiple.sqlc,
           checks/draw_exceeds_the_supply.sqlc,
           checks/clearance_with_unserved.sqlc,
           checks/unresolved_part.sqlc,
           checks/leaf_reached_twice.sqlc,
           checks/coupling_does_not_attenuate.sqlc,
           checks/narrows_a_point_value.sqlc,
           checks/range_says_no_range.sqlc,
           checks/bound_fell_with_no_range.sqlc,
           checks/window_lost_or_summed.sqlc,
           checks/derived_slack_over_a_window.sqlc,
           checks/window_not_applicable_on_a_rate.sqlc,
           checks/elimination_not_applicable_with_parts.sqlc,
           checks/fusion_sum_disagrees.sqlc,
           checks/local_part_dangles.sqlc,
           checks/local_cycle.sqlc,
           checks/denied_remainder_is_not_contradicted.sqlc,
           checks/one_part_fusion_alters_its_part.sqlc)
